  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nDestabilization is under way across the region in the wake of a\ndecaying morning convective complex, with the most robust \nclearing located across southern areas. The strongest \nconvective cores will graze portions of southwest Virginia and \nthe southern coalfields where SPC has added a slight risk of\nsevere storms. Additional scattered to numerous showers and \nthunderstorms are already developing along a slow-moving \nsurface cold front pressing southeastward across the Middle Ohio\nValley.\n\nThe thermodynamic environment remains exceptionally moist,\ncharacterized by precipitable water values approaching 2.0\ninches, which sits well above seasonal climatology. Soundings\ndisplay long, skinny instability profiles with Mixed-Layer CAPE\nreaching 800 to 1200 J/kg, up to 1500 J/kg across southern\ncounties. These soundings feature deep warm cloud depths\nexceeding 12000 feet, supporting highly efficient warm rain\nproduction with rainfall rates of 1 to 2 inches per hour. Flow \nat H700 around 30 to 35KTs out of the west will keep individual\nconvective cores moving at a modest pace. However, low-level \nconvergence along the cold front as it sinks slowly to the \nsoutheast runs nearly parallel to the mid-level flow, which may \npromote cell training over the same drainages. \n\nDeep-layer bulk shear of 25 to 30KT will favor a predominantly \nmulticellular storm mode. Low-level hodographs show modest \ncurvature, yielding effective storm-relative helicity of 70 to \n100 m2/s2. This shear profile could foster brief updraft \nrotation in any semi-discrete cells, maintaining a low, though \nnon-zero, threat for a brief tornado. While the primary severe \nwind potential remains concentrated along our southern border \nwhere instability is maximized, antecedent saturated ground \nconditions mean that even sub-severe wind gusts of 35 to 45 mph \ncan uproot exposed trees.\n\nGiven severely compromised soils from repeated heavy rainfall\nover the preceding days, 3 hour flash flood guidance is \ngenerally between 1.0 and 1.5 inches across much of the forecast\narea. High precipitation rates will result in rapid runoff into\nsmall streams. A Flash Flood Watch remains active through 2 AM \nEDT Tuesday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nIn the wake of the cold frontal passage late tonight, surface\nwinds will diminish to 5 mph or less under weak northwesterly\nflow. While we should maintain at least some mid-level cloud\ncover, will likely see surface temperatures falling to\nsaturation by early Tuesday morning. Widespread low stratus and\nfog is expected expected to form over much of the area heading \ninto Tuesday morning which could impact the morning commute.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nA brief period of dry weather will take hold Tuesday afternoon\nthrough most of Wednesday as high pressure builds across the\nCentral Appalachians and column moisture decreases behind the\ncold front. Precipitable water values drop to around 1.0 to 1.2\ninches on Tuesday, allowing small streams and creeks an\nopportunity to recede. However, subsurface soil moisture will\nremain nearly saturated, leaving infiltration capacity severely\ndegraded. \n\nAtmospheric moisture will pool northward again late Wednesday\ninto Wednesday night ahead of the next upper-level shortwave\ntrough and cold front. Precipitable water values rise back\ntoward 1.8 inches by Thursday morning. Upper-level dynamics will\nstrengthen as the right rear quadrant of an H200 jet streak\npositions over the Middle Ohio Valley, providing broad synoptic\nscale ascent. With modest diurnal destabilization expected\nThursday afternoon, scattered to numerous showers and\nthunderstorms will redevelop along the boundary. High\ninstantaneous rainfall rates falling on saturated soils will\npresent a renewed flash flood risk, supporting the Weather\nPrediction Center Marginal Risk of Excessive Rainfall for\nThursday. \n\nThe broad synoptic pattern through the late week and upcoming\nweekend will be dominated by strong mid-level ridging over the\nwestern United States and longwave troughing centered over the\neastern tier. This configuration will keep the Central\nAppalachians in northwest flow aloft, directing periodic\nshortwaves and secondary cold fronts through the region every\ncouple of days.",
